<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Irina Proskurina
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">irina.proskurina@univ-lyon2.fr</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Université Lumière Lyon 2
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Université Claude Bernard Lyon 1
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ERIC, 69007 Lyon, France
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guillaume Metzler
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Université Lumière Lyon 2
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Université Claude Bernard Lyon 1
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ERIC, 69007 Lyon, France
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Julien Velcin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>École Centrale de Lyon
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>LIRIS, CNRS UMR 5205, France
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">High memory demands of generative language models have drawn attention to quantization, which reduces computational cost, memory usage, and latency by mapping model weights to lower<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>precision integers.
Approaches such as GPTQ effectively minimize input-weight product errors during quantization; however, recent empirical studies show that they can increase biased outputs and degrade performance on fairness benchmarks, and it remains unclear which specific weights cause this issue.
In this work, we draw new links between quantization and model fairness by adding explicit group<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>fairness constraints to the quantization objective and introduce <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fair<span id="abstract1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ</span>, the first quantization method explicitly designed to reduce unfairness in large language models.
The added constraints guide the learning of the rounding operation toward less<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>biased text generation for protected groups.
Specifically, we focus on stereotype generation involving occupational bias and discriminatory language spanning gender, race, and religion.
Fair<span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ has minimal impact on performance, preserving at least 90% of baseline accuracy on zero<span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>shot benchmarks, reduces unfairness relative to a half<span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>precision model, and retains the memory and speed benefits of 4<span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text">-</span>bit quantization.
We also compare the performance of Fair<span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ with existing debiasing methods and find that it achieves performance on par with the iterative null<span id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_text">-</span>space projection debiasing approach on racial<span id="abstract1.1.11" class="ltx_text">-</span>stereotype benchmarks.
Overall, the results validate our theoretical solution to the quantization problem with a group<span id="abstract1.1.12" class="ltx_text">-</span>bias term, highlight its applicability for reducing group bias at quantization time in generative models, and demonstrate that our approach can further be used to analyze channel<span id="abstract1.1.13" class="ltx_text">-</span> and weight<span id="abstract1.1.14" class="ltx_text">-</span>level contributions to fairness during quantization.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autoregressive (causal) language models have demonstrated strong efficacy across a variety of conditional generative tasks - for example, generating well-formed answers to user-posed questions in scientific and general-knowledge domains, as well as performing sentence-completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mostafazadeh et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Recent studies show that attaining state-of-the-art performance in these tasks generally requires scaling up both the volume of training data and the number of trainable parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15206v1/fair-gptq-v4.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/224;" width="310" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Illustration of the proposed method Fair-GPTQ. Fair-GPTQ quantizes models using paired data consisting of stereotypical inputs <math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and anti-stereotypical inputs <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It rounds the weights to minimize the difference in model behavior between these paired examples. The Hessian in Fair-GPTQ modifies the original GPTQ optimization by incorporating a term that captures the difference between paired data:
<math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}=-2\alpha\mathbf{W}\Delta\mathbf{X}_{01}\Delta\mathbf{X}_{01}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>01</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}=-2\alpha\mathbf{W}\Delta\mathbf{X}_{01}\Delta\mathbf{X}_{01}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{X}_{01}=\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{X}_{01}=\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to different quantized weights.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As model scale grows to meet accuracy demands, post hoc compression techniques such as quantization are adopted to control memory and computation overhead.
Quantization is one approach to encoding model weights in lower-precision formats, reducing memory consumption and accelerating model inference, thereby making high-capacity models both faster and more practical for real-world deployment. Post-training quantization methods can be categorized into round-to-nearest methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2022b)</a></cite>, which simply map values to the nearest quantization codebook entry, and methods such as GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2022)</a></cite>, which treat quantization as an optimization problem, minimizing the reconstruction error of the input-weight product.
Recent studies have shown that quantization can amplify biases already present in large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gonçalves and Strubell (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohammadshahi et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
These include stereotypical associations, hallucinated content, and unfair treatment of protected groups.
However, existing works evaluate such biases only before and after quantization, without considering fairness as an explicit factor <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">within</span> the quantization process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we address this gap by introducing <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fair<span id="S1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ</span>, a quantization approach that integrates group<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>fairness constraints to reduce bias during quantization.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it modifies the GPTQ optimization problem by adding a pair<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>difference term to the objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our contributions</span> are as follows:
(i) We propose a modification to the GPTQ optimization objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2022)</a></cite> by introducing a regularization term that controls model bias during quantization (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).
Our ablation studies demonstrate that this modification challenges the common assumption, originally made in OBS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun et al. (1989)</a></cite>, that pre-trained weights lie in a local minimum.
(ii) We introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fair-GPTQ</span>, a fairness-aware quantization scheme designed to reduce bias during quantization (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The Fair-GPTQ Algorithm ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
(iii) We analyze the column matrix and layer depth levels of the weight updates in <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fair-GPTQ</span>, identifying common patterns across two model families (§<a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Comparison to GPTQ ‣ 5 Results ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>).
(iv) We evaluate the performance of <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fair-GPTQ</span> using quantization and debiasing benchmarks, demonstrating its effectiveness (§<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Comparison to Debiasing Methods ‣ 5 Results ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM Quantization</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantization reduces the numerical precision of neural network weights and activations, and is widely adopted in natural language processing to accelerate the inference of large language models and reduce memory consumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Compared to pruning and distillation, quantization preserves the original network topology, allowing LLMs to remain compatible with tensor-parallel execution and optimized matrix multiplication kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chee et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gugger et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Recent works on quantization have focused on post-training quantization methods that require only small calibration corpus, enabling practical model compression after pretraining while avoiding the high computational cost of full retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nagel et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubara et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
Early work employs round-to-nearest (RTN) quantization with varying granularities for zero-point and scale computation.
LLM.int8() adopts channel-wise quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2022a)</a></cite>, while ZeroQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2022)</a></cite> and AdaDim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heo et al. (2023)</a></cite> use group-wise channel quantization, which requires less storage for scale and zero-point values.
However, both approaches lead to significant accuracy degradation at 4-bit precision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2022a)</a></cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2022)</a></cite> reformulate quantization as an optimization problem and introduce GPTQ, which compensates for quantization errors using the inverse Hessian matrix.
Specifically,the weight reconstruction error is minimized on calibration data <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> layer-wise:
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{c}=\underset{\mathbf{W}^{\prime}}{\arg\min}\|\mathbf{W}\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{X}\|_{2}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐖</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{c}=\underset{\mathbf{W}^{\prime}}{\arg\min}\|\mathbf{W}\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{X}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the matrix of quantized weights. This approach is inspired by <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Optimal Brain Damage</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun et al. (1989)</a></cite>, which suggests that the impact of removing or modifying a parameter (saliency) can be estimated using the Hessian of the loss function without model retraining.
Building on this principle, GPTQ applies compensation to remaining weights during quantization and achieves superior performance compared to RTN methods on zero-shot evaluation benchmarks for LLMs.
However, because it optimizes only for reconstruction accuracy, GPTQ can unintentionally increase group<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>targeting biases, which we aim to reduce with <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fair<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs and Group Bias</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work on social bias in LLMs addresses group<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>agnostic and group<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>specific biases, emphasizing the spread of stereotypes about protected groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caliskan et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Davidson et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Omrani et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Early works on bias mitigation and aligning representations include removing biased directions in embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bolukbasi et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manzini et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordia and Bowman (2019)</a></cite>, fine-tuning or continued pre-training on balanced counterfactual data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zmigrod et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steed et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and projecting out biased subspaces from latent representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ravfogel et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al. (2021)</a></cite> have introduced <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Self-Debias</span> approach that adjusts a pre-trained model’s output probabilities guided by a textual (prompted) description of the unwanted content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A line of recent empirical studies has investigated the general performance decline on zero-shot benchmarks due to quantization errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2022a)</a></cite>.
A few studies have demonstrated that quantization increases bias, impacting factual reliability, harmful generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaiswal et al. (2023)</a></cite>, hallucinated translation outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohammadshahi et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and increased stereotyped likelihood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gonçalves and Strubell (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al. (2023)</a></cite>, with particularly pronounced effects on multilingual models, especially those using non-Latin scripts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marchisio et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on quantization and social bias related research, we try to solve the issue highlighted in empirical studies that measure the impact of quantization and introduce <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fair-GPTQ</span>, an adaptation of the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OBS</span> framework that accounts for group bias during quantization, specifically targeting unequal stereotype generation toward particular groups, including gender, race, and religion.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fair-GPTQ</span> algorithm, which accounts for group generalization (stereotyping) bias during quantization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start by reformulating the optimization problem in GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2022)</a></cite> and OBS, introducing a group<span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>bias constraint.
Specifically, we define bias toward a particular group characterized by a sensitive attribute (such as gender, religion, or race) as the difference in likelihood assigned to generated text conditioned on different attribute values, for instance, <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“He is good at math”</span> versus <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“She is good at math”</span>.
We consider two matrices <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{0},\mathbf{X}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{0},\mathbf{X}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> representing a pair of input texts of length <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> that differ only in a single protected<span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>attribute token.
For instance, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the embedding for the word <em id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">she</em>, while <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the embedding for <em id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">he</em> in the same context.
Here, <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding (and hidden) dimension and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence length.
We further denote by
<math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> the full<span id="S3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>precision weight matrix of a language model layer, and by <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> its quantized counterpart.
In transformer feed<span id="S3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text">-</span>forward layers, <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4d</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the expansion from hidden dimension <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4d</annotation></semantics></math> in the intermediate projection, while <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d</annotation></semantics></math> for self<span id="S3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text">-</span>attention projections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Quantization with Bias Awareness</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To make the quantization step sensitive to potential stereotypes, we introduce a <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bias penalty</em> that measures how much the quantized model changes the representation gap between the
stereotyped (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) and anti<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>stereotyped (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) inputs.
Formally, this can be restated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{c}=\underset{\mathbf{W}^{\prime}}{\arg\min}\left(\|\mathbf{W}\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{X}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}\right.\left.+\|\mathbf{W}\mathbf{X}_{1}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{X}_{1}\|_{2}^{2}+\alpha\|\mathbf{W}^{\prime}(\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1})\|_{2}^{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐖</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{c}=\underset{\mathbf{W}^{\prime}}{\arg\min}\left(\|\mathbf{W}\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{X}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}\right.\left.+\|\mathbf{W}\mathbf{X}_{1}-\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{X}_{1}\|_{2}^{2}+\alpha\|\mathbf{W}^{\prime}(\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1})\|_{2}^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, for the sake of clarity, we set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{W}^{\prime}-\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{W}^{\prime}-\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let us also denote by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector), the flattened weight matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">row<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise</em>,
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{\prime}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{\prime}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{w}^{\prime}-\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{w}^{\prime}-\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\bigl[\underbrace{\,W_{1,1},\ldots,W_{1,d}}_{\mathbf{w}_{1}},\;\ldots,\;\underbrace{W_{n,1},\ldots,W_{n,d}}_{\mathbf{w}_{n}}\,\bigr]^{\!\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munder><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></munder></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\bigl[\underbrace{\,W_{1,1},\ldots,W_{1,d}}_{\mathbf{w}_{1}},\;\ldots,\;\underbrace{W_{n,1},\ldots,W_{n,d}}_{\mathbf{w}_{n}}\,\bigr]^{\!\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We use row<span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise flattening because the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) couples parameters only <em id="S3.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within the same row</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{i}=W_{i,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{i}=W_{i,:}</annotation></semantics></math> (the loss terms are sums of in<span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>row squares). Consequently, the cross<span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>row second derivatives vanish, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}f}{\partial\mathbf{w}_{i}\,\partial\mathbf{w}_{k}^{\!\top}}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}f}{\partial\mathbf{w}_{i}\,\partial\mathbf{w}_{k}^{\!\top}}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq k</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the objective function in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) we aim to optimize. Thus, the Hessian in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>space is block<span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>diagonal by rows. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hassibi et al. (1993)</a></cite>, a second<span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>order Taylor expansion of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) around <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{\prime}=\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{\prime}=\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(\mathbf{w}^{\prime})\;=\;f(\mathbf{w})+\bigl(\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}\bigr)^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\mathbf{w}^{\prime})\;=\;f(\mathbf{w})+\bigl(\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}\bigr)^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\,\Delta\mathbf{w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the (matrix) gradient and input<span id="S3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>space Hessian are</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\alpha\,\mathbf{W}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}^{\!\top}\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\alpha\,\mathbf{W}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}^{\!\top}\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2\bigl(\mathbf{X}_{0}\mathbf{X}_{0}^{\!\top}+\mathbf{X}_{1}\mathbf{X}_{1}^{\!\top}+\alpha\,\Delta\mathbf{X}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}^{\!\top}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2\bigl(\mathbf{X}_{0}\mathbf{X}_{0}^{\!\top}+\mathbf{X}_{1}\mathbf{X}_{1}^{\!\top}+\alpha\,\Delta\mathbf{X}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}^{\!\top}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{d\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{J})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{J})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Hessian in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>space (row<span id="S3.SS1.p2.4.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise vectorization) is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\;=\;\mathbf{H}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{n}\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{nd\times nd}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\;=\;\mathbf{H}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{n}\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{nd\times nd}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Contrary to standard GPTQ and OBQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar and Alistarh (2022)</a></cite>, we keep the objective’s gradient <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}</annotation></semantics></math> non<span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>zero at the pretrained weights (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{w}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{w}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) so that it captures the bias signal induced by the paired inputs <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{0},\mathbf{X}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{0},\mathbf{X}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using the OBS framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun et al. (1989)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hassibi et al. (1993)</a></cite> with row<span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise vectorization <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate the objective by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\Delta\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}}\;\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\Delta\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}}\;\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\,\Delta\mathbf{w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Originally, this approach was designed for <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pruning</span>, where weights were set to zero via constraints.
Here, following OBQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar and Alistarh (2022)</a></cite>, we adapt it to quantization by imposing the elementwise constraint</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}\;=\;\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-w_{q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>quant</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}\;=\;\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-w_{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{1,\ldots,nd\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{1,\ldots,nd\}</annotation></semantics></math> indices an entry of the row<span id="S3.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise flattened vector <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>th element of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>quant</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is its quantized value, and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>th standard basis vector. This leads to the constrained problem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\Delta\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\Delta\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}^{\!\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,\Delta\mathbf{w}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\,\Delta\mathbf{w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}\;=\;\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-w_{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>quant</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{w}\;=\;\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-w_{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> are, respectively, the gradient vector and Hessian with respect to the row<span id="S3.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise flattened <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
The solution to Eq. (<a href="#S3.Ex4" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is given in Proposition <a href="#Thmmyprop1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmyprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_myprop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmyprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmmyprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmyprop1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmyprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmyprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the optimization problem in Eq. (<a href="#S3.Ex4" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) (with row<span id="Thmmyprop1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise flattening <math id="Thmmyprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math>), the solution is</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbf{w}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbf{w}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}-\frac{\,w_{q}-\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}}{[\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}]_{qq}}\;\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{e}_{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>quant</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}-\frac{\,w_{q}-\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}}{[\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}]_{qq}}\;\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{e}_{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmyprop1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmyprop1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmmyprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="Thmmyprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmmyprop1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>th standard basis vector and <math id="Thmmyprop1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}]_{qq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}]_{qq}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="Thmmyprop1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmmyprop1.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>th diagonal element of <math id="Thmmyprop1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmyprop1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmyprop1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmyprop1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The corresponding change of the quadratic objective is</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta f</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\bigl(w_{q}-\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}\bigr)^{2}}{2\,[\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}]_{qq}}-\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>quant</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\bigl(w_{q}-\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})-\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}\bigr)^{2}}{2\,[\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}]_{qq}}-\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Proposition ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. It involves deriving the Lagrangian of the constrained problem and solving for <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The Fair<span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ Algorithm</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow GPTQ and perform quantization <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">column<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise</em>, motivated by the structure of the Hessian <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\;=\;\mathbf{H}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}\;=\;\mathbf{H}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Since the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) depends only on the weights within each row <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{i,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i,:}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessian with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>vec</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vec_{r}}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> is block<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>diagonal, meaning that each row is independent, yet all rows share the same input<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>space Hessian block <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By performing the update column-wise, the adjustment is computed once for a given column and applied across all rows simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, regarding the weight update, the first term of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) represents the main update applied uniformly to all weights and is independent of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
The numerator of the second term rearranges as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{q}-\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}})-\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>quant</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{q}-\mathbf{e}_{q}^{\!\top}\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}})-\operatorname{quant}(w_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the second term corrects the difference between this target and the quantized value by adjusting the remaining weights. This observation allows us to separate the <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">debiasing update</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}})</annotation></semantics></math> from the subsequent quantization main loop of the GPTQ algorithm.
In matrix form, the debiasing update is:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\;\leftarrow\;\mathbf{W}-(\mathbf{H}^{-1}\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}\mathbf{W}^{\!\top})^{\!\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\;\leftarrow\;\mathbf{W}-(\mathbf{H}^{-1}\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}\mathbf{W}^{\!\top})^{\!\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}=2\alpha\,\Delta\mathbf{X}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}^{\!\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}=2\alpha\,\Delta\mathbf{X}\,\Delta\mathbf{X}^{\!\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Because the inverse Hessian <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is block<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>diagonal,
multiplying <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{w}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> amounts to
applying <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> independently to the contribution of each row of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This row<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>wise operation can be written compactly as
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\mathbf{H}^{-1}\,\mathbf{J}^{\!\top}\bigr)^{\!\top}\;=\;\mathbf{J}\,\mathbf{H}^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\mathbf{H}^{-1}\,\mathbf{J}^{\!\top}\bigr)^{\!\top}\;=\;\mathbf{J}\,\mathbf{H}^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math>
yielding the final matrix form of the correction. We illustrate the multiplication below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.Ex6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic"><span id="S3.Ex6.1.1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_nicematrix-placeholder"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">nicematrix-placeholder: </span><span id="S3.Ex6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">pNiceArray (nicematrix)</span></span></span></span><span id="S3.Ex6.1.2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_nicematrix-placeholder"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">nicematrix-placeholder: </span><span id="S3.Ex6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">pNiceArray (nicematrix)</span></span></span></span></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> vector is composed from rows of matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As in GPTQ, the inverse <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed once and reused throughout quantization. We use a block size of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}128</annotation></semantics></math> and a symmetric quantization scheme.
The weight matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is divided into groups of consecutive columns <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each group a symmetric scaling factor <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed, serving as the quantization scale for that group.
The zero point is set to zero, as required in symmetric quantization mode.
Each column <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{:,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{:,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is quantized as <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{:,j}\;=\;\operatorname{clip}\!\left(\operatorname{round}\!\left(\tfrac{\mathbf{W}_{:,j}}{s_{g}}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mpadded style="width:2.487em;" width="2.487em"><mi>round</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{:,j}\;=\;\operatorname{clip}\!\left(\operatorname{round}\!\left(\tfrac{\mathbf{W}_{:,j}}{s_{g}}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we compute the quantization error, the fraction of the second term in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 3.1 Quantization with Bias Awareness ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), which serves as a correction to compensate for the quantization in the subsequent weights.
That error is further used to update the remaining not yet quantized columns inside the same block with the corresponding slice of the Hessian.
The quantization is done until all blocks are quantized. We summarize the full procedure in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 The Fair-GPTQ Algorithm ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Fair-GPTQ: Bias-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In Fair<span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>GPTQ, we introduce an additional bias term into the objective function, while keeping all matrix dimensions and operations identical to GPTQ.
The overall runtime is unchanged and continues to be dominated by the <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\!\times\!d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!\times\!d</annotation></semantics></math> inverse update.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Fair-GPTQ: Quantize <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> with fairness-aware Hessian and blocksize <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\leftarrow\mathbf{0}_{n\times d},\mathbf{E}\leftarrow\mathbf{0}_{n\times B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐐</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟎</mn><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐄</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟎</mn><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\leftarrow\mathbf{0}_{n\times d},\mathbf{E}\leftarrow\mathbf{0}_{n\times B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{acc}}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}_{0}\mathbf{X}_{0}^{\!\top}+\mathbf{X}_{1}\mathbf{X}_{1}^{\!\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">acc</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{acc}}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}_{0}\mathbf{X}_{0}^{\!\top}+\mathbf{X}_{1}\mathbf{X}_{1}^{\!\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}\leftarrow 2\alpha(\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1})(\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">bias</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}\leftarrow 2\alpha(\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1})(\mathbf{X}_{0}-\mathbf{X}_{1})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}\leftarrow\mathbf{H}_{\text{acc}}+\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">acc</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">bias</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}\leftarrow\mathbf{H}_{\text{acc}}+\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\leftarrow\mathbf{W}-(\mathbf{H}^{-1}\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}\mathbf{W}^{\top})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐇</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">bias</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐖</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\leftarrow\mathbf{W}-(\mathbf{H}^{-1}\mathbf{H}_{\text{bias}}\mathbf{W}^{\top})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
